WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
Performance under pressure is different from performance in calm conditions.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:10.908
Pressure may come from time limits, uncertainty, consequence, audience, conflict, or the knowledge that a decision matters.

3
00:00:10.908 --> 00:00:22.221
At a general level, pressure can sharpen focus for some tasks, but it can also narrow attention, increase rushing, and reduce decision quality if it is not managed.

4
00:00:22.221 --> 00:00:26.261
The goal is not to eliminate every sign of pressure.

5
00:00:26.261 --> 00:00:28.281
Real work often contains pressure.

6
00:00:28.281 --> 00:00:35.553
The goal is performance stability: the ability to keep observing, reasoning, communicating, and acting in a useful way.

7
00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:37.977
Simple pre-performance routines can help.

8
00:00:37.977 --> 00:00:48.482
A performer may review the objective, define the first action, check required resources, and decide in advance when to slow down, escalate, or ask for support.

9
00:00:48.482 --> 00:00:52.926
Practical pause techniques can also help at a non-clinical level.

10
00:00:52.926 --> 00:01:01.006
Taking a brief breath, naming the next decision, or asking what evidence is missing can interrupt panic and tunnel vision.

11
00:01:01.006 --> 00:01:03.430
These techniques are not medical treatment.

12
00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:11.914
They are ordinary performance habits that create a small space between pressure and action, especially when the urge is to rush.

13
00:01:11.914 --> 00:01:17.167
Expert performers know when speed is useful and when slowing down is safer.

14
00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:22.823
If the situation is familiar and the action is reversible, speed may be appropriate.

15
00:01:22.823 --> 00:01:31.711
If the stakes are high, the information is unclear, or the action is hard to undo, a deliberate pause can improve quality.

16
00:01:31.711 --> 00:01:39.791
Stability under pressure comes from preparation, routines, team support, and the willingness to adjust pace when the situation demands it.
